/// \defgroup grp_w2_update_modules_misc Update modules: prone timer, battle plans and radius decals
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Three small game-logic update modules: ProneUpdate (a prone timer
///        fed by damage), BattlePlanUpdate (Zero Hour's battle-plan building
///        logic, still present in BFME 2) and RadiusDecalUpdate (keeps a
///        ground marker alive on its object).
///
/// All three are update modules: an Object gets one when its INI definition
/// has a `Behavior = ProneUpdate` (or `BattlePlanUpdate`, `RadiusDecalUpdate`)
/// line followed by a module tag. ModuleFactory::init registers each name
/// with two factories, one that builds the module's data from the INI block
/// and one that builds the module itself for each new object. The module
/// data is shared by every object made from the same template; the module
/// holds the per-object state and lives as long as its object.
///
/// Other code reaches these modules by name: it asks the object for the
/// update module registered as, for example, "ProneUpdate", casts it to the
/// concrete class and uses its public members. These include
/// ProneUpdate::goProne, BattlePlanUpdate::getActiveBattlePlan and
/// RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal; AttackNugget::create also sets
/// RadiusDecalUpdate's kill-when-no-longer-attacking flag right after
/// calling createRadiusDecal on the same module (Zero Hour: through the
/// public setter killWhenNoLongerAttacking). Those members are not part of
/// any shared module interface. RadiusDecalUpdate::killRadiusDecal is public
/// too but has no known caller. The prone-effect and turret helpers are
/// protected, so only the module itself calls them, and \c update is
/// reached through the update-module interface.
///
/// Each module's \c update runs inside the game logic. Its return value
/// (UpdateSleepTime) says when to run it again: UPDATE_SLEEP_NONE means on
/// the next logic frame, UPDATE_SLEEP_FOREVER means not until something wakes
/// the module with UpdateModule::setWakeFrame. \ref page_frame_loop explains
/// logic frames.
///
/// The modules' logic counters and flags are lockstep state: in a
/// multiplayer game every machine runs the same modules on the same frames
/// and must reach the same values. BattlePlanUpdate's and
/// RadiusDecalUpdate's BFME 2 \c xfer functions write their state into the
/// save game (\ref sub_save_load_crc_xfer); ProneUpdate's BFME 2 save format
/// is not confirmed here. RadiusDecalUpdate does not save its decal record
/// at all: loading a game releases the decal and marks the record empty
/// (RadiusDecal::clear sets the record's empty flag). None of the members
/// documented here draws a random number.
///
/// The class pages describe each module as a whole; the function blocks
/// cover individual members. The bodies follow Zero Hour's (GeneralsMD
/// GameLogic/Object/Update/ProneUpdate.cpp, BattlePlanUpdate.cpp and
/// RadiusDecalUpdate.cpp), reached in part through the BFME 1
/// reconstruction, with the BFME 2 differences noted where they occur. How
/// much of each class is recovered changes daily;
/// \ref page_reading_code_ledger explains how to check.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The module names are fixed in code: an Object can use these three
///   modules and set their INI fields, but cannot add a module of its own
///   (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - The INI fields each module reads are listed on its class page. They
///   come from the retail executable's own field tables, not from Zero Hour.
/// - Damage, player commands and object creation lists decide when these
///   modules act. Each class page names the trigger it has in BFME 2, where
///   that is known.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Counts kept in logic frames, such as the prone timer, depend on the
///   logic rate. Fields read as durations are written in milliseconds and
///   converted to frames when the INI file is loaded; ProneUpdate's ratio
///   is not.
/// - RadiusDecalUpdate holds a handle to a client-side decal from inside
///   the logic. A new renderer has to keep the decal record's "empty" flag
///   independent of what is drawn (see RadiusDecalUpdate).
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref page_modding_modules


/// \class ProneUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w2_update_modules_misc
/// \brief Update module that keeps an object prone for a number of logic
///        frames proportional to the damage it takes, then lets it get up.
///
/// The module keeps one counter: the logic frames the object has left to
/// stay prone. ProneUpdate::goProne adds to it and switches the prone
/// effects on when the counter first becomes positive; ProneUpdate::update
/// counts it down once per logic frame and switches the effects off when it
/// reaches zero. The effects are two flags on the Object:
/// - the PRONE model condition, which the object's draw module can match to
///   show a prone animation or model;
/// - the NO_ATTACK object status. Zero Hour: Object::isAbleToAttack refuses
///   an object with this status.
///
/// In BFME 2 the only known caller of ProneUpdate::goProne is
/// AIUpdateInterface::privateGoProne, which finds the object's module
/// registered as "ProneUpdate" and does nothing if there is none. Zero Hour:
/// privateGoProne runs for the AI's go-prone command, which
/// AICommandInterface::aiGoProne issues; SlavedUpdate::onSlaverDamage and
/// MobMemberSlavedUpdate::onSlaverDamage send it to a slave when its
/// master is damaged, and AIGroup::groupGoProne sends it to a whole group.
/// Which code issues the command in BFME 2 is not established here.
///
/// \par Lifetime and state
/// The module factory creates one ProneUpdate per object that lists it; the
/// constructor starts the counter at zero. The counter is the module's only
/// state. Zero Hour's \c xfer function for this module saves it after the
/// UpdateModule base, at version 1. This page does not confirm BFME 2's save
/// format for the module.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - INI: the retail field table for this module has a single field,
///   \c DamageToFramesRatio, a real number (default 1.0 in BFME 2's module
///   data constructor). It is the number of logic frames of prone time per
///   point of damage taken. Fields read as durations (for example
///   BattlePlanUpdate's \c ...Time fields) are converted from time to frames
///   when loaded; this ratio is not, so it is tied to the logic rate.
/// - Damage is cut to a whole number before it is multiplied, so a hit of
///   less than one point adds nothing, and the product is truncated when it
///   is added to the counter.
/// - The look of a prone unit comes from a draw-module condition state that
///   includes PRONE. The name is in BFME 2's model-condition name table.
/// - A ratio of zero or less means the object never goes prone: the counter
///   can never become positive, so the prone effects never start. This
///   assumes the damage dealt by a hit is never negative.
///
/// \note BFME 2 keeps the model-condition flags on the Object itself. Zero
///       Hour's version changes them on the object's Drawable instead.
/// \see AIUpdateInterface::privateGoProne, Object::setStatus

/// \fn UpdateSleepTime ProneUpdate::update(void)
/// \brief Counts the prone time down by one logic frame and ends the prone
///        state when it runs out.
///
/// The game logic calls this through the object's update-module list. If
/// the counter is positive it is decremented, and when that makes it zero
/// ProneUpdate::stopProneEffects clears the PRONE model condition and the
/// NO_ATTACK status. A counter that is already zero or negative is left
/// alone.
///
/// \return Always UPDATE_SLEEP_NONE, so the module runs on every logic
///         frame for the object's whole life, prone or not.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body: Zero Hour's ProneUpdate::update, unchanged. No direct caller is
/// known because it is reached through the update interface's virtual
/// table.
/// \see ProneUpdate::goProne, ProneUpdate::stopProneEffects

/// \fn void ProneUpdate::goProne(const DamageInfo *damageInfo)
/// \brief Adds prone time for one hit and starts the prone state if the
///        object was not already prone.
///
/// AIUpdateInterface::privateGoProne calls this (Zero Hour: when the AI
/// receives its go-prone command). The added time is the damage the hit
/// actually dealt, cut to a whole number, times the module data's
/// \c DamageToFramesRatio. Further
/// hits while prone extend the time; only the first one that makes the
/// counter positive calls ProneUpdate::startProneEffects.
///
/// \param damageInfo The damage record of the hit. Only the damage actually
///                   dealt is read. Must not be null.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body: Zero Hour's ProneUpdate::goProne. The caller is a BFME 2 fact:
/// AIUpdateInterface::privateGoProne is the only known retail caller.
/// \see ProneUpdate::update, AIUpdateInterface::privateGoProne

/// \fn void ProneUpdate::startProneEffects(void)
/// \brief Sets the PRONE model condition and the NO_ATTACK status on the
///        module's object.
///
/// Called only from ProneUpdate::goProne, when the object becomes prone.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// BFME 2 sets the condition bit directly in the Object's model-condition
/// flags and, only when the bit was not already set, calls the Object's
/// model-condition notifier. That notifier hands the Object's flags to its
/// Drawable and wakes the object's AI; BFME 1's reconstruction calls its
/// counterpart notifyModelConditionChanged. The status goes through
/// Object::setStatus with the set flag true. Both indices are checked
/// against BFME 2's name tables: PRONE in the model-condition names,
/// NO_ATTACK in the object-status names.
///
/// \note Zero Hour calls Drawable::setModelConditionState on the object's
///       drawable instead. BFME 2, like BFME 1, keeps the flags on the
///       Object and passes them on to the Drawable.
/// \see ProneUpdate::stopProneEffects, Object::setStatus

/// \fn void ProneUpdate::stopProneEffects(void)
/// \brief Clears the PRONE model condition and the NO_ATTACK status on the
///        module's object.
///
/// The mirror of ProneUpdate::startProneEffects, called only from
/// ProneUpdate::update when the counter reaches zero. The condition bit is
/// cleared, with the same change notification only when it was set, and
/// Object::setStatus clears the status.
///
/// \see ProneUpdate::startProneEffects, Object::setStatus


/// \class BattlePlanUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w2_update_modules_misc
/// \brief Zero Hour's Strategy Center logic: a building switches between
///        three battle plans with timed pack and unpack transitions, and the
///        active plan gives bonuses to its owner's army.
///
/// Zero Hour: it is a SpecialPowerUpdateModule, an update module that also
/// implements the special-power update interface. It works together with
/// the object's own SpecialPowerModule for the template named by
/// \c SpecialPowerTemplate, and ignores commands for any other template.
/// The player picks Bombardment, Hold the Line or Search and Destroy with a
/// command button whose option bits name the plan (OPTION_ONE, OPTION_TWO,
/// OPTION_THREE). Only an active plan affects the army: the bonuses (an
/// armor scalar for Hold the Line, a sight-range scalar for Search and
/// Destroy, weapon bonuses per plan) go to the owner's units that match the
/// valid and invalid KindOf masks, and units are briefly paralyzed when the
/// plan changes.
///
/// The module moves through four transition states. BFME 2's per-frame
/// transition body and its status-setting function (Zero Hour:
/// BattlePlanUpdate::update and BattlePlanUpdate::setStatus) follow Zero
/// Hour's sequence:
/// - idle: once the idle time (\c TransitionIdleTime) has passed and a plan
///   has been chosen, the module starts unpacking that plan;
/// - unpacking: lasts the plan's animation time, then the plan becomes
///   active; for Bombardment the building's turret is enabled;
/// - active: lasts until the player chooses a different plan. The module
///   then starts packing, except from Bombardment: there it first idles the
///   AI and turns the turret back to its rest position, and packs, with the
///   turret disabled, only once the turret is there;
/// - packing: lasts the plan's animation time, then the module goes idle.
///
/// Each state except idle sets its own model condition on the building
/// (Zero Hour: the DOOR_1 to DOOR_3 opening, waiting-to-close and closing
/// conditions), and unpacking and packing set the drawable's animation loop
/// to the plan's animation time, so the building's draw module plays the
/// transition. On entering unpacking, BFME 2, like Zero Hour, also posts a
/// radar event and shows the plan's message label as an in-game message.
///
/// Zero Hour keeps a \c VisionObjectName field and a createVisionObject
/// function, but its only call is commented out; the source says the reveal
/// moved to the thing template's ShroudRevealToAllRange, so Zero Hour never
/// creates the object. BFME 2 differs: when the module enters the unpacking
/// state, its status-setting function calls a body that, unless the module
/// has already recorded a vision object ID, creates one from the template
/// named by \c VisionObjectName at the building's position, sets its
/// shroud-clearing range and records its ID. The test looks only at the
/// recorded ID, not at whether that object still exists, and the module's
/// code never clears the ID, so it creates at most one vision object.
/// BFME 1's reconstruction makes the same call at the same point.
///
/// BFME 2 still registers the module as "BattlePlanUpdate", and its field
/// table has Zero Hour's keys. Whether any shipped BFME 2 object definition
/// uses it is not established here, because the game's INI data is not part
/// of this repository.
///
/// \par Lifetime and state
/// One instance per object that lists the module. The constructor
/// allocates the module's bonuses record, and the module keeps the ID of
/// its vision object. Zero Hour: the module keeps an audio event for each
/// pack, unpack, idle and announcement sound. BFME 2 keeps instead
/// reference-counted references to audio entries, four for each kind of
/// sound and indexed by plan, plus the handle of the one sound it is
/// playing. The references are looked up by name when the INI file is
/// loaded. A slot with no reference plays nothing. Every status change
/// first stops that sound; unpacking then starts the plan's unpack sound
/// and announcement, active the plan's idle loop if one is set, and
/// packing the plan's pack sound. The destructor also stops that sound,
/// through the same audio-manager call.
///
/// The module's object-created hook (Zero Hour: onObjectCreated) does its
/// setup only when the module data names a \c SpecialPowerTemplate. Then it
/// finds the object's special-power module for that template, copies the
/// audio references from the module data, sets a weapon-set flag on the
/// object, sets a weapon lock if the object has an AI, and disables the
/// turret. Without a template it only marks the module's settings invalid;
/// the per-frame transition body then returns at once on every frame, so
/// the building never starts a transition. Zero Hour's onObjectCreated and
/// update depend on the template in the same way.
///
/// BFME 2's \c xfer saves, after the UpdateModule base and a version 1
/// marker: the displayed plan, the desired plan, the plan affecting the
/// army, the transition status, the next frame a transition may happen, the
/// invalid-settings and turret-centering flags, the bonuses (armor scalar,
/// the three plan counters, sight scalar, both KindOf masks) and the vision
/// object's ID. It then passes the Xfer and the playing-sound handle to an
/// audio-manager function. What that function does with the handle is not
/// established here, so this page does not say whether the playing sound
/// itself is saved. In light-CRC mode (Xfer::IsLightCRC,
/// \ref sub_save_load_crc_crc) it returns right after the base and skips
/// all of this.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - INI: the retail field table has these keys, in Zero Hour's order:
///   \c SpecialPowerTemplate; \c BombardmentPlanAnimationTime,
///   \c HoldTheLinePlanAnimationTime, \c SearchAndDestroyPlanAnimationTime
///   and \c TransitionIdleTime (durations, read with
///   INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt); for each plan an unpack and a pack
///   sound name, a message label and an announcement name, plus
///   \c SearchAndDestroyPlanIdleLoopSoundName; \c ValidMemberKindOf and
///   \c InvalidMemberKindOf; \c BattlePlanChangeParalyzeTime;
///   \c HoldTheLinePlanArmorDamageScalar and
///   \c SearchAndDestroyPlanSightRangeScalar; the four
///   \c StrategyCenter... fields (a sight scalar, a detects-stealth flag, a
///   max-health scalar and its change type); and \c VisionObjectName.
/// - \c SpecialPowerTemplate must be set: without it the building never
///   starts a transition, so no plan ever becomes active (see "Lifetime and
///   state").
/// - BFME 2 looks up each sound and announcement name in the audio manager
///   when the INI file is loaded and stores a reference to the entry it
///   finds; Zero Hour read them as plain strings. An unknown name is a load
///   error ("Invalid Sound"), and the value \c NoSound leaves the slot
///   empty. The message labels and \c VisionObjectName are still plain
///   strings. Each message label is looked up in the game's string table
///   when its plan starts unpacking.
/// - The \c ...Time fields are written in milliseconds and converted to
///   logic frames, rounded up, when the INI file is loaded.
/// - Fixed in code (Zero Hour): there are exactly three plans, they are
///   chosen by the command button's first three option bits, and which
///   kind of bonus each plan grants is decided by code; the data only sets
///   the scalars, the masks and the timings.
/// \see \ref page_core_frameworks_upgrades

/// \fn BattlePlanStatus BattlePlanUpdate::getActiveBattlePlan(void) const
/// \brief Returns the plan that is currently affecting the army, or
///        PLANSTATUS_NONE while the building is not in the active state.
///
/// The displayed plan and the plan affecting the army differ during
/// transitions; this getter reports the latter, and only once unpacking
/// has finished. Idle, unpacking and packing all read as PLANSTATUS_NONE.
/// Zero Hour: ControlBar::getCommandAvailability uses it to disable
/// (restrict) a stop button that has OPTION_ONE set unless Bombardment is
/// the active plan. In BFME 2 its one direct caller looks up the object's
/// module registered as "BattlePlanUpdate" and compares the result with
/// Bombardment, the same test as Zero Hour's stop-button check.
///
/// \return PLANSTATUS_BOMBARDMENT, PLANSTATUS_HOLDTHELINE or
///         PLANSTATUS_SEARCHANDDESTROY while active; PLANSTATUS_NONE
///         otherwise.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body: the BFME 1 reconstruction's getter, byte-identical in BFME 2,
/// itself Zero Hour's. The test is for the third transition state, which is
/// TRANSITIONSTATUS_ACTIVE in Zero Hour's enumeration.
/// \see BattlePlanUpdate::isTurretInNaturalPosition

/// \fn void BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret(void)
/// \brief Orders the turret used by the object's current weapon back to its
///        rest position.
///
/// Gets the object's AI, asks AIUpdateInterface::getWhichTurretForCurWeapon
/// which turret the current weapon uses, and calls
/// AIUpdateInterface::recenterTurret for it. An object with no AI, or whose
/// current weapon has no turret, is left alone.
///
/// Its one caller is the module's per-frame transition body (Zero Hour:
/// BattlePlanUpdate::update). When the player has chosen a new plan while
/// Bombardment is active and the turret is not yet at rest, that body puts
/// the AI into idle, calls this once, and remembers that centering has
/// started so that it is not ordered again. BFME 2's body and Zero Hour's
/// agree on this.
///
/// \see BattlePlanUpdate::isTurretInNaturalPosition,
///      BattlePlanUpdate::enableTurret

/// \fn Bool BattlePlanUpdate::isTurretInNaturalPosition(void)
/// \brief Reports whether the turret used by the object's current weapon is
///        at its rest position.
///
/// Same lookup as BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret, then
/// AIUpdateInterface::isTurretInNaturalPosition for that turret.
///
/// \return true only if the object has an AI, the current weapon has a
///         turret and that turret is at rest. No AI or no turret reads as
///         false.
///
/// Its one caller is the module's per-frame transition body (Zero Hour:
/// BattlePlanUpdate::update), the same one that calls
/// BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret.
///
/// \note In BFME 2 as in Zero Hour, the module packs up from Bombardment
///       only once this returns true, and the transition body checks this
///       only when the object has an AI. An object with no AI never leaves
///       Bombardment. One whose current weapon has no turret stays in
///       Bombardment for as long as that is so: the body asks for the
///       recentering only once and then waits, frame by frame, for this
///       function to return true.
/// \see BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret

/// \fn void BattlePlanUpdate::enableTurret(Bool enable)
/// \brief Enables or disables the turret used by the object's current
///        weapon.
///
/// Same lookup as BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret, then
/// AIUpdateInterface::setTurretEnabled. Nothing happens without an AI or a
/// turret.
///
/// When the module data names a \c SpecialPowerTemplate, the module
/// disables the turret when its object is created; without one it never
/// calls this function (see BattlePlanUpdate). It enables the turret when
/// Bombardment becomes active and disables it again when it packs up from
/// Bombardment. In BFME 2 the callers are the module's object-created
/// hook (in Zero Hour, its onObjectCreated override) and its per-frame
/// transition body (Zero Hour: BattlePlanUpdate::update).
///
/// \param enable true to let the turret aim and turn, false to hold it.
/// \see BattlePlanUpdate::recenterTurret,
///      BattlePlanUpdate::isTurretInNaturalPosition


/// \class RadiusDecalUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w2_update_modules_misc
/// \brief Update module that owns one radius decal, a marker drawn on the
///        ground around a point, keeps it animated while it exists, and can
///        remove it when its object stops attacking.
///
/// Other code finds the module by name and calls
/// RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal. The known BFME 2 caller is
/// AttackNugget::create, the object creation list entry that makes an
/// object attack a position. If the object has an AI, it locks the weapon
/// slot and orders the attack; separately, if the object has a
/// RadiusDecalUpdate, it creates the nugget's delivery decal at the target
/// and sets the module's kill-when-no-longer-attacking flag. The marker
/// then disappears as soon as the attack ends. Zero Hour's AttackNugget
/// does the same.
///
/// While a decal exists, RadiusDecalUpdate's \c update runs every logic
/// frame. Once the kill-when-no-longer-attacking flag is set and the object
/// no longer has the IS_ATTACKING status, it releases the decal and sleeps
/// until woken; otherwise it calls RadiusDecal::update, which animates the
/// decal.
///
/// \par Lifetime and state
/// The constructor empties the decal record, sets the
/// kill-when-no-longer-attacking flag to false and puts the module to sleep
/// until a decal is created. The destructor empties the decal record, which
/// releases its render object. BFME 2's \c xfer writes, after the
/// UpdateModule base and a version 1 marker, only the
/// kill-when-no-longer-attacking flag. The decal record's own transfer
/// stores nothing; when a game is loaded it releases the decal and marks the
/// record empty.
/// Zero Hour: RadiusDecal::xferRadiusDecal is the same unimplemented stub.
/// A loaded game therefore shows no delivery decal until a new one is
/// created. In light-CRC mode (Xfer::IsLightCRC) the function returns right
/// after the base, as BattlePlanUpdate's does.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The module has no INI fields of its own. In BFME 2 the module factory
///   registers it with a generic module-data factory that unrelated modules
///   share (WallUpgradeUpdate and BoneFXDamage, for example); Zero Hour's
///   field table for it is empty too.
/// - Add it to an object that fires an object creation list's \c Attack
///   entry if that entry's decal should show. The look comes from the
///   entry: \c DeliveryDecal (a decal block) and \c DeliveryDecalRadius.
///   Zero Hour's entry also has \c NumberOfShots and \c WeaponSlot; all four
///   key names are in BFME 2's executable.
/// - BFME 2's decal block has more keys than Zero Hour's: \c Texture,
///   \c Texture2, \c Style, \c OpacityMin, \c OpacityMax,
///   \c OpacityThrobTime (read as a plain number in BFME 2),
///   \c RotationsPerMinute, \c Color, \c OnlyVisibleToOwningPlayer,
///   \c MinRadius, \c MaxRadius, \c MaxSelectedUnits and
///   \c SpiralAcceleration.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The decal record holds a render-side decal, which the template creates
/// only if the local player may see it (OnlyVisibleToOwningPlayer), and a
/// separate "empty" flag. RadiusDecalTemplate::createRadiusDecal marks the
/// record non-empty whenever there is an owning player, the template has a
/// texture and the radius is positive, before it checks whether the local
/// player sees the decal; BFME 2's body and Zero Hour's agree on this.
/// RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal reads the flag to decide whether to
/// wake the module. In Zero Hour that is the only game-logic code that
/// reads it; InGameUI::handleRadiusCursor also reads it, for the
/// client-side radius cursor. A renderer that tied the flag to visibility
/// would therefore make machines schedule this module differently.
/// \see RadiusDecalTemplate::createRadiusDecal, RadiusDecal::clear

/// \fn void RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal(const RadiusDecalTemplate &tmpl, Real radius, const Coord3D &pos)
/// \brief Replaces the module's decal with a new one made from a template,
///        and wakes the module if the new decal is not empty.
///
/// Clears any decal the module already holds, then has
/// RadiusDecalTemplate::createRadiusDecal build a new one at \p pos with
/// \p radius, owned by the object's controlling player, whose colour is
/// used when the template gives none. Finally it sets the
/// module's wake frame: the next logic frame if the decal is non-empty, so
/// that \c update starts animating it, or never if it is empty.
///
/// \param tmpl   Decal template, usually a \c DeliveryDecal block from an
///               object creation list entry.
/// \param radius Decal radius in world units. A radius of zero or less
///               gives an empty decal.
/// \param pos    Centre of the decal in world coordinates.
///
/// \note The wake test reads the decal record's empty flag (Zero Hour:
///       RadiusDecal::isEmpty). An empty decal sends the module to sleep
///       forever; a non-empty one wakes it on the next logic frame. BFME 1's
///       reconstruction reads the same flag.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Body: Zero Hour's RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal. The caller is a
/// BFME 2 fact: AttackNugget::create is the only known retail caller.
/// \see RadiusDecalUpdate::killRadiusDecal, AttackNugget::create

/// \fn void RadiusDecalUpdate::killRadiusDecal(void)
/// \brief Removes the module's decal and puts the module to sleep until a
///        new decal is created.
///
/// RadiusDecal::clear releases the decal and marks the record empty, then
/// UpdateModule::setWakeFrame sends the module to sleep with
/// UPDATE_SLEEP_FOREVER. It is safe to call when there is no decal. No direct BFME 2 caller has been found, and none
/// of Zero Hour's sources calls it either.
///
/// \see RadiusDecalUpdate::createRadiusDecal
